<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
		<Description>The VpnHood app as a UI sees it, for any UI toolkit: the app's state and API in one place, the words of every language read from the assets folder, and the code that turns the app's state into text - errors, dates, traffic, documents. No toolkit, no theme, no picture, no file of its own</Description>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- The web UI's src/services and src/helpers, in C#: what a page needs that is not drawing.
	     Avalonia, MAUI or anything else builds its pages on this and brings its own toolkit; nothing
	     here references one. Nothing here references an asset package either: the files - the
	     pictures and the words alike, locales/<culture>.json - are named here and read through the
	     Toolkit's IAssetProvider the head hands in (a folder in process, the app's web host from a
	     browser page), so which package ships them is the presentation's choice, and a brand is a
	     package swap. The one thing generated from a store
	     is Strings.g.cs - the KEYS of its en.json, which every store must have words for - written
	     by _sync-assets.ps1 in VpnHood.AppUi.Assets.Classic. -->

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- The app's API, and the whole of what this package knows about the app: the six
		     interfaces and the shapes they carry, in process or over HTTP. No engine, no listener. -->
		<ProjectReference Include="..\..\AppLib\Api\Api\VpnHood.AppLib.Api.csproj" />
		<ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Common\VpnHood.Core.Common.csproj" />
		<!-- IAssetProvider: the store's files, wherever this platform reads them from. -->
		<ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Toolkit\VpnHood.Core.Toolkit.csproj" />
	</ItemGroup>
</Project>
